-- title:
--   p. 16 — a ball of radius k is at most n times the part of the ball of radius k + n − 1 inside a subgroup of index n
-- statement:
--   Let $G$ be generated by a finite set $S$ and let $H$ be a subgroup of finite index $n$. Writing
--   $B_S(k)$ for the ball of radius $k$, for every $k \ge 0$
--
--   $$|B_S(k)| \le n \cdot |B_S(k + n - 1) \cap H|.$$
--
--   It lets the growth of a group be controlled by the growth of a subgroup of finite index.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Balls are the published `Chou.wordBall` (products of at most $k$
--   elements of $S \cup S^{-1}$) and sizes are `Nat.card`. The source states this for a subgroup of
--   index $n$; `H.FiniteIndex` makes $n$ finite and nonzero.
-- source:
--   A. Garrido, "An introduction to amenable groups", lecture notes, Oxford Advanced Class in Algebra, Michaelmas 2013 (PDF, Feb 2015), p. 16, the proof of Theorem 4.9; https://web.archive.org/web/20260805000803/https://www.math.uni-duesseldorf.de/~garrido/amenable.pdf

